@page "/reports"
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = SamplePermissions.ReportsReadPolicy)]

@* The policy name is never registered anywhere. The package's FronteggPolicyProvider materializes any policy
   whose name starts with "perm:" (or "permid:") on demand, so declaring the constant is the whole setup.

   The permission *attributes* — [PermissionAuthorize], [PermissionIdAuthorize] — would do nothing here: they
   are IAuthorizationFilters, and only the MVC action-invocation pipeline runs those. Policies are the surface
   that works everywhere, which is why a Blazor app uses them. *@

<PageTitle>Reports</PageTitle>

<h1>Reports</h1>

<p>
    You hold <code>@SamplePermissions.ReportsRead</code>. A user without it never reaches this markup —
    <code>AuthorizeRouteView</code> renders its <code>NotAuthorized</code> fragment instead.
</p>

<p>
    Nothing on this page is real reporting; the point is that the gate above the <code>@@page</code> directive
    is the only thing standing between an authenticated user and this content.
</p>
